Nested monorepo paths are relative to app working directory; all tracked shell/hook paths resolve LF.
Review result: no actionable introduced defect found. The only changed production mechanism is Git attributes; no shell syntax or executable modes change. Existing tracked shell entrypoints all selected and pinned. Baseline attr assertions and actual hook execution fail; fixed clone passes both. Optional stripped files skip scanning and deleted .sh paths still resolve LF by pattern. No security risk surface or unrelated preexisting finding identified. Full Flutter suite delegated to owner acceptance run; no concurrent heavy suite started.
Additional scope assessment: the tier-1 test explicitly requires tracked .githooks/commit-msg and scripts/dev/create_feature.sh and requires Git metadata. An adopter deliberately removing either artifact or distributing only an archive must adjust this repository-contract test, consistent with existing tooling tests. No supported strip variant removes those artifacts, so this introduces no strip regression. In nested repositories the test restricts git ls-files to the app subdirectory and evaluates attributes relative to that directory; independent nested probe passes. Existing tests already impose Git metadata and tracked file contracts. Extensionless future shell entrypoints outside hooks must gain their own attribute pattern: the test detects them through their shebang and rejects unspecified eol, which is the intended maintenance gate.
